  "refuter_findings": [
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:1",
      "id": "ex-coordinate-projections-and-inclusions-on-product-banach-spaces",
      "location": "Example",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Example assumes only “Let X and Y be Banach spaces” and claims all four coordinate maps “are bounded linear operators of norm 1.” Take X={0} and Y=R. Then pi_X:X×Y→X and iota_X:X→X×Y are zero operators, hence have operator norm 0, not 1. Step 2.1 itself relies on testing with a nonzero x∈X, which is not assumed.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "09e6f97be3c9acfedb64de2174408d2a4b7b636540e3f417f608ce9e4f6dff81"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:2",
      "id": "ex-coordinate-projections-and-inclusions-on-product-banach-spaces",
      "location": "Verification 3.1",
      "defect": "ill-formed",
      "evidence": "Step 3.1 states “pi_X,pi_Y ∈ B(X×Y,X) and iota_X,iota_Y ∈ B(X,X×Y).” But pi_Y(x,y)=y has codomain Y, not X, and iota_Y has domain Y, not X. These memberships are ill-typed unless X=Y, an unstated special case.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "09e6f97be3c9acfedb64de2174408d2a4b7b636540e3f417f608ce9e4f6dff81"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:3",
      "id": "thm-extension-of-a-bounded-map-from-a-dense-subspace",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The Statement assumes only a dense subspace and Banach target. But Proof 1.1 says “for each n≥1 choose d_n(x)∈D” and explicitly calls this “the selected AC_omega step.” The current def-countable-choice defines AC_omega as supplying a choice function for every N-indexed family of nonempty sets, and records that it is stronger than ZF. No AC_omega (or stronger) hypothesis is stated.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "13425d42f5a487994689835f9a05350c6136bce6e08d962f1234981af9598520"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:4",
      "id": "thm-quotient-of-banach-by-closed-subspace-is-banach",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The proof constructs a countable family of representatives: Proof 2.1 says “For each k, choose u_k∈X representing ξ_(n_{k+1})−ξ_(n_k).” These are independently chosen elements of countably many nonempty representative sets, exactly the choice-function assertion in def-countable-choice. The Statement assumes only that X is Banach and M closed, with no AC_omega or stronger choice hypothesis.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "fc3de8a3a59be67750e96a80f69d83672ed7795f63f1205fd52cc669a1e19fbe"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:5",
      "id": "ex-quotient-by-the-kernel-is-isometric-to-the-range-with-the-induced-quotient-norm",
      "location": "Verification 2.2",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The Example calls ||Tx||_quot an “induced quotient norm,” but its facts establish only that M=ker T is a linear subspace ([L3]). The scoped theorem thm-quotient-seminorm-is-a-norm-iff-subspace-is-closed says the quotient seminorm is a norm exactly when M is closed. Boundedness of T does imply ker T is closed, but this needed inference is neither proved nor cited before the norm claim.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "9b9cb03db691877015c54897ac9f4bb5c5697e54ffa0c4d87b2fcf0bfd236f06"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:6",
      "id": "thm-bounded-right-inverse-iff-kernel-is-complemented",
      "location": "Proof 2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Proof 1.1 defines P=I_X−ST and proves P²=P, but never establishes that P is bounded. Step 2.1 then invokes [L2], whose stated criterion is that complemented subspaces are ranges of bounded projections. Boundedness follows immediately from boundedness of S and T, but it is absent from the argument used to apply [L2].",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "ed5b5639aec6c26fdafb7c62298c2630736c52793b47e741c6c825dfc1619a27"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:5:7",
      "id": "thm-bounded-left-inverse-iff-range-is-complemented",
      "location": "Proof 2.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "After defining P=TS, Proof 2.1 proves P²=P and identifies its range, then says [L2] shows the range complemented. [L2] requires a bounded projection, but the proof never derives that TS is bounded from the given bounded operators T and S. This is an elementary but missing inference.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "28cc0b1458b1936b5ec584858d2d198fd0166ed5db4a89a22de5ab745a606d8b"
    }
